WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.550
Fast enough to play along.

2
00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.982
The last of five short videos on how

3
00:00:03.982 --> 00:00:04.831
Continuo works.

4
00:00:08.763 --> 00:00:09.447
The last question.

5
00:00:09.674 --> 00:00:11.079
By the time a machine knows what you

6
00:00:11.079 --> 00:00:12.560
played, has the moment to play along

7
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:13.053
already gone?

8
00:00:13.281 --> 00:00:13.737
Let's count.

9
00:00:13.965 --> 00:00:15.521
A sixteenth note at a hundred and twenty

10
00:00:15.521 --> 00:00:16.926
beats per minute lasts a hundred and

11
00:00:16.926 --> 00:00:17.875
twenty-five milliseconds.

12
00:00:18.103 --> 00:00:19.659
That is the smallest step the band plays.

13
00:00:19.887 --> 00:00:21.520
Here it is, drawn as one bar, with the

14
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.355
pluck at its left end.

15
00:00:22.583 --> 00:00:24.253
Everything that happens to one note, from

16
00:00:24.253 --> 00:00:25.924
the string to the speakers, is laid along

17
00:00:25.924 --> 00:00:27.556
it, and each step carries a label saying

18
00:00:27.556 --> 00:00:28.126
how we know it.

19
00:00:28.507 --> 00:00:29.379
Start at the pluck.

20
00:00:29.654 --> 00:00:31.674
The chip has to notice the note, and wait

21
00:00:31.674 --> 00:00:33.097
to hear how hard it was played.

22
00:00:33.373 --> 00:00:35.347
By the chip's design, the note leaves it

23
00:00:35.347 --> 00:00:37.183
about forty-five milliseconds after the

24
00:00:37.183 --> 00:00:37.963
string is struck.

25
00:00:38.239 --> 00:00:40.258
That is a design value: the number the

26
00:00:40.258 --> 00:00:41.085
chip was built to.

27
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.334
Nobody has yet measured it on the hardware

28
00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:45.492
itself, so it is drawn pale, with a dashed

29
00:00:45.492 --> 00:00:45.721
edge.

30
00:00:46.147 --> 00:00:48.596
Down the cable to the laptop: about one

31
00:00:48.596 --> 00:00:49.249
millisecond.

32
00:00:49.575 --> 00:00:51.371
Measured, so it is drawn solid.

33
00:00:51.698 --> 00:00:53.929
It is so thin on this bar you can barely

34
00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:54.310
see it.

35
00:00:54.778 --> 00:00:55.854
Then the band decides.

36
00:00:56.148 --> 00:00:58.006
The drummer typically takes under two

37
00:00:58.006 --> 00:00:59.963
milliseconds for a sixteenth, and the

38
00:00:59.963 --> 00:01:01.968
bassist about one more: about three

39
00:01:01.968 --> 00:01:02.408
together.

40
00:01:02.702 --> 00:01:04.903
Measured, on the seventeenth of October,

41
00:01:04.903 --> 00:01:05.588
and solid too.

42
00:01:05.881 --> 00:01:07.838
The band's own thinking is the cheapest

43
00:01:07.838 --> 00:01:08.425
step of all.

44
00:01:08.921 --> 00:01:11.110
Getting a sound out of the laptop and into

45
00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.044
the speakers takes about twenty-three

46
00:01:13.044 --> 00:01:13.706
milliseconds.

47
00:01:14.012 --> 00:01:16.150
That one is reported: it is what the

48
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.186
laptop's audio driver says about itself.

49
00:01:18.492 --> 00:01:20.121
We checked it together with the

50
00:01:20.121 --> 00:01:22.513
microphone, but never on its own, so it is

51
00:01:22.513 --> 00:01:23.735
pale and dashed as well.

52
00:01:24.208 --> 00:01:26.335
And the whole thing, from the pluck to the

53
00:01:26.335 --> 00:01:28.321
sound you hear, has never been measured

54
00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:28.841
end to end.

55
00:01:29.125 --> 00:01:30.968
Nobody has yet put a microphone by the

56
00:01:30.968 --> 00:01:32.812
guitar and another by the speakers and

57
00:01:32.812 --> 00:01:33.474
timed the gap.

58
00:01:33.758 --> 00:01:35.838
So there is no total on this bar, and you

59
00:01:35.838 --> 00:01:37.351
will not hear one in this video.

60
00:01:37.634 --> 00:01:39.431
We would rather show you the gap than

61
00:01:39.431 --> 00:01:39.714
guess.

62
00:01:43.297 --> 00:01:44.896
Imagine a band that listened, and then

63
00:01:44.896 --> 00:01:45.208
reacted.

64
00:01:45.442 --> 00:01:47.274
Here are two sixteenths, side by side, and

65
00:01:47.274 --> 00:01:48.678
you pluck at the start of the first.

66
00:01:48.912 --> 00:01:50.472
That band hears the note only after the

67
00:01:50.472 --> 00:01:51.993
chip has reported it and the cable has

68
00:01:51.993 --> 00:01:53.748
carried it, and it decides, and by those

69
00:01:53.748 --> 00:01:55.464
numbers, by the time its own sound is out

70
00:01:55.464 --> 00:01:57.219
of the laptop, most of the sixteenth would

71
00:01:57.219 --> 00:01:57.843
already be gone.

72
00:01:58.077 --> 00:01:59.793
Its drums and bass would land late, every

73
00:01:59.793 --> 00:02:00.651
time, on every note.

74
00:02:00.885 --> 00:02:02.523
You would hear a band dragging behind you.

75
00:02:02.943 --> 00:02:04.104
Continuo's band does not react.

76
00:02:04.328 --> 00:02:05.601
Same two sixteenths, same pluck.

77
00:02:05.826 --> 00:02:07.473
Before each sixteenth, it decides what to

78
00:02:07.473 --> 00:02:08.933
play in it, from everything you have

79
00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:10.280
played so far, this note included.

80
00:02:10.505 --> 00:02:12.152
The decision is made before the slot, and

81
00:02:12.152 --> 00:02:13.762
the drums and bass are placed right on the

82
00:02:13.762 --> 00:02:15.072
line, where the sixteenth begins.

83
00:02:15.296 --> 00:02:16.943
It never plays instantly, because nothing

84
00:02:16.943 --> 00:02:17.168
could.

85
00:02:17.393 --> 00:02:18.179
It plays on the beat.

86
00:02:18.403 --> 00:02:20.163
So it is always one note ahead of you: by

87
00:02:20.163 --> 00:02:21.623
the time you hear it, it has already

88
00:02:21.623 --> 00:02:22.409
decided the next one.

89
00:02:25.963 --> 00:02:26.607
Does it work?

90
00:02:26.904 --> 00:02:29.131
On the seventeenth of October, Sena played

91
00:02:29.131 --> 00:02:31.210
eight takes through the whole chain:

92
00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:33.536
guitar, pickup, board, chip, laptop,

93
00:02:33.536 --> 00:02:33.982
speakers.

94
00:02:34.279 --> 00:02:36.506
Drums in every take, and bass as well from

95
00:02:36.506 --> 00:02:37.050
the fourth.

96
00:02:37.347 --> 00:02:39.327
This is the laptop's page during one of

97
00:02:39.327 --> 00:02:39.575
them.

98
00:02:39.872 --> 00:02:41.802
Ninety-nine point eight percent of his

99
00:02:41.802 --> 00:02:43.485
notes reached the band before the

100
00:02:43.485 --> 00:02:45.415
sixteenth they belonged to was decided.

101
00:02:45.712 --> 00:02:46.999
In time, in other words.

102
00:02:47.296 --> 00:02:49.523
Thirty-one notes, out of thirteen thousand

103
00:02:49.523 --> 00:02:51.602
one hundred and nineteen, arrived after

104
00:02:51.602 --> 00:02:52.741
their slot was decided.

105
00:02:53.038 --> 00:02:54.225
That number is measured.

106
00:02:54.522 --> 00:02:56.502
The laptop logs when every note arrives

107
00:02:56.502 --> 00:02:58.631
and when every sixteenth is decided, and

108
00:02:58.631 --> 00:03:00.709
this is what those logs say for the eight

109
00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:01.006
takes.

110
00:03:02.502 --> 00:03:04.041
And the thirty-one that were late?

111
00:03:04.312 --> 00:03:06.122
When a note arrives after its sixteenth

112
00:03:06.122 --> 00:03:08.113
has already been decided, it simply waits

113
00:03:08.113 --> 00:03:09.833
for the next slot, and counts there.

114
00:03:10.104 --> 00:03:12.005
The band does not stop, and it does not

115
00:03:12.005 --> 00:03:12.774
rush to catch up.

116
00:03:13.046 --> 00:03:14.222
The band never plays late.

117
00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:16.349
A sound that would be late is not played

118
00:03:16.349 --> 00:03:18.340
at all; the next one lands on its beat.

119
00:03:20.057 --> 00:03:22.158
One last thing, to be clear about what

120
00:03:22.158 --> 00:03:23.081
these numbers are.

121
00:03:23.388 --> 00:03:25.080
Three kinds went into this video.

122
00:03:25.387 --> 00:03:27.796
The cable and the decision were measured:

123
00:03:27.796 --> 00:03:30.051
timed, on the real system, with logs to

124
00:03:30.051 --> 00:03:30.666
show for it.

125
00:03:30.974 --> 00:03:33.024
The sound out of the laptop is a number

126
00:03:33.024 --> 00:03:34.972
the audio driver reports about itself.

127
00:03:35.279 --> 00:03:37.329
The chip's forty-five milliseconds is a

128
00:03:37.329 --> 00:03:39.226
design number, and it has not been

129
00:03:39.226 --> 00:03:40.712
measured on the hardware yet.

130
00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.224
And the time from a pluck to the sound you

131
00:03:43.224 --> 00:03:45.274
hear has never been measured end to end.

132
00:03:45.581 --> 00:03:47.478
That is still owed, and when it is

133
00:03:47.478 --> 00:03:49.169
measured, it will be said here.

134
00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:53.339
That is Continuo: a band that hears every

135
00:03:53.339 --> 00:03:55.785
string, finds each note as it starts, and

136
00:03:55.785 --> 00:03:57.752
decides the next sixteenth before it

137
00:03:57.752 --> 00:03:58.178
happens.

138
00:03:58.497 --> 00:03:59.241
Come and play.

